"Refinement (2026-08-12, user): COMBINATIONS AND STATUS EFFECTS. (1) lassoed+prone differs from lassoed+standing -- and the model must NOT author pairwise combinations. It does not: the evaluation context is the MERGE of all active modifiers' provides/consumes (body + armaments + stance + every relation + every status effect). Prone provides different properties than standing; the lasso's consumption subtracts from either; the resulting action sets differ automatically. N relations x M stances never needs NxM authoring -- composition is the whole point of the counted vocabulary. (2) User: some of these are maybe STATUS EFFECTS that restrict actions. Yes -- third lifecycle kind, same vocabulary. Taxonomy: all three are CONTEXT MODIFIERS contributing provides/consumes to the single evaluation, distinguished ONLY by lifecycle: STANCE = exclusive-one-active, ends by swap or forced transition; RELATION = has a target entity/object ref, ends when the counterpart dies/is destroyed or via escape techniques (authored exits); STATUS EFFECT = no counterpart dependency, ends by duration/cleanse/condition (entangled-by-spell = status; lassoed-by-rope = relation, because a destructible counterpart exists -- the classification test is WHAT DETERMINES ITS LIFETIME). Status effects may also carry periodic effects (poison ticks); the action-restriction facet is the part that plugs into the property vocabulary, orthogonal to ticking. (3) Parameterized consumption: a relation/status instance states WHICH slots it consumes (chain-the-arm consumes hand x1 vs chain-the-leg consumes gait), chosen by the applying technique. The availability layer never cares which modifier kind restricted it -- it reads one merged context."